---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Aside from '../../../components/Aside.astro';
import {funktorSidebar} from '../../../data/funktor-sidebar';
import {Code} from 'astro:components';
import {dep, kotlinVersion} from '../../../data/site';
---

<DocsLayout title="Getting Started" sidebar={funktorSidebar} description="Set up a Funktor application">

    <h1>Getting Started</h1>

    <p class="text-xl text-gray-400 !mt-2 !mb-8">
        Set up a Funktor application from scratch.
    </p>

    <h2>Add the dependency</h2>

    <p>The easiest way to get all modules is the <code>funktor-all</code> meta-dependency. Or pick individual modules.
    </p>

    <Code code={`// build.gradle.kts
dependencies {
    // All modules at once
    implementation("${dep.funktorAll}")

    // Or pick what you need
    implementation("${dep.funktorCore}")
    implementation("${dep.funktorRest}")
    implementation("${dep.funktorAuth}")
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <h2>Define your app</h2>

    <p>A Funktor app starts with <code>funktorApp</code>, typed by your config class. It reads the config Ktor
        loads, builds your Kontainer blueprint from it, and then starts the server, or with <code>--cli</code> runs a
        CLI command instead.</p>

    <Code code={`val app = funktorApp<MyConfig>(
    kontainers = { config -> AppKontainers(createBlueprint(config)) },
)

fun main(args: Array<String>) = app.run(args)`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <h2>Define your Kontainer blueprint</h2>

    <p>Services are registered in <a href="/ultra/kontainer/">Kontainer</a> modules. <code>funktor(...)</code>
        registers the Funktor modules (the code generator is a separate dependency, <code>funktor-codegen</code>, added with
        <code>funktorCodegen()</code>); each one's block picks its storage backend. You add your application services
        alongside them.</p>

    <Code code={`fun createBlueprint(config: MyConfig) = kontainer {
    funktor(
        config = config,
        rest = { jwt() },           // needs funktor.auth.jwt in the config
        auth = { useKarango() },
    )

    karango(config = config.arangodb)

    module(MyAppModule)
}

val MyAppModule = module {
    // Your services
    singleton(UserRepository::class)
    dynamic(UserService::class)

    // Your API feature
    singleton(MyApiFeature::class)
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <h2>Define API routes</h2>

    <p>Routes use the typed API system from the <a href="/ultra/funktor/rest">REST module</a>. Define endpoints with
        type-safe parameters, request bodies, and response types. Every group declares who may reach it.</p>

    <Code code={`class MyApi : ApiRoutes("my-api", authFloor = { public() }) {

    val getUsers = MyApiClient.GetUsers.mount {
        docs { name = "List users" }
        .handle {
            val users = userService.listAll()
            ApiResponse.ok(users)
        }
    }
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <h2>Configure the server</h2>

    <p>The Ktor server module installs the auth providers, a kontainer per request, and mounts the routes of every
        <code>ApiFeature</code> in the kontainer.</p>

    <Code code={`fun Application.module() = app.module(this) { app, config, init ->
    install(SSE)
    install(CORS) {
        // your CORS config
    }

    authentication {
        jwtCaller(AUTH_JWT)
        anonymous(AUTH_ANON)
    }

    routing {
        // A kontainer per request, carrying the caller
        installKontainer { call ->
            app.kontainers.create { with { call.currentUserProvider() } }
        }

        authenticate(AUTH_JWT, AUTH_ANON) {
            init.getAll(ApiFeature::class)
                .flatMap { it.getRouteGroups() }
                .flatMap { it.all }
                .forEach { handle(it) }
        }
    }
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <Aside>The mounting loop is yours, not the framework's. Funktor finds every <code>ApiFeature</code> in the
        kontainer (for boot validation, the API access matrix and the code generator); where its routes go is up to
        the app.
    </Aside>

    <div class="mt-12">
        <a href="/ultra/funktor/core"
           class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 rounded-lg bg-brand-400/10 text-brand-400 border border-brand-400/30 hover:bg-brand-400/20 transition-all font-medium">
            Core Module &rarr;
        </a>
    </div>

</DocsLayout>
